\title{From Imagetext to Worldtext: Generative AI as Operative Ekphrasis}
\author{Watson Hartsoe \and Jay David Bolter}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Prompting research currently treats the prompt as a disposable string of words. That assumption is now mis-specified: in contemporary \enquote{promptcraft,} the operative unit is increasingly a compiled workflow---distributed across interfaces, images, code, and iterative constraints---that does not merely request an image but stages a world. This creates an anomaly at the center of both media theory and HCI: our dominant vocabulary still describes ekphrasis as representation, while our dominant evaluation regimes still score prompting as task completion, even as generative systems migrate toward immersive, navigable simulations. The result is a growing critical blind spot: the more seamless world models become, the more their world-building decisions---cultural weights, defaults, exclusions---become illegible.

We name this condition \operativeekphrasis\ \citep{Bajohr2024OperativeEkphrasis} and extend Mitchell's \imagetext\ into the \worldtext: a probabilistic, interactive simulation in which language functions as a handle on scene, object, and physics generation \citep{Mitchell1994PictureTheory}. We define \thickprompting\ as the generative corollary to Geertz's \enquote{thick description}---directionally reversed, because description now calls artifacts into being---yet equally dependent on layered cultural codes, here absorbed as model weights \citep{Geertz1993InterpretationCultures}. To make this operational rather than merely theoretical, we examine Homer's Shield of Achilles as an emblematic world-description and prototype a workflow that moves from ekphrastic text to \imagetext\ to \worldtext. Finally, we juxtapose Homer's generative cosmology with Auden's Cold War overwriting of the shield to show how \worldtexts\ encode historical mood, bureaucratic structure, and violence---whether authored by poets or by interfaces that pretend not to author at all \citep{Auden1955ShieldOfAchillesBook}.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Prompting is being evaluated with the wrong unit of analysis. In most HCI and \enquote{prompt engineering} discourse, the prompt is treated as a user-authored sentence and the output as a recoverable target: better prompting equals higher accuracy, tighter control, fewer errors \citep{LiuChilton2022DesignGuidelinesCHI}. That paradigm held---briefly---when generation mostly meant a single image.

However, an inconsistent reality has taken over practice: \enquote{promptcraft} now behaves less like writing a caption and more like compiling a media pipeline. The prompt is stretched across iterative differentials, reference images, style taxonomies, reusable templates, and executable code that selects, splices, and re-injects text fragments into generation loops \citep{Oppenlaender2024TaxonomyPromptModifiers,OppenlaenderLinderSilvennoinen2025PromptingAIArt,BradeEtAl2023Promptify,AlmedaEtAl2024DreamSheets}. When the prompt becomes a workflow, accuracy metrics do not merely fall short; they actively misread what is happening. They score the surface string while the real agency migrates into the hidden machinery that structures variation.

This misreading matters because generative systems are not stopping at \imagetext. World-model research and commercial 3D generators are normalizing navigable, physics-bearing environments---systems that effectively treat language as an interface to world construction \citep{HaSchmidhuber2018WorldModels,BruceEtAl2024Genie,DeepMind2025Genie3Blog,WorldLabs2025MarbleBlog}. The field's current prompt vocabulary---string in, image out---cannot track this shift. It leaves critique and governance chasing artifacts after the fact, while the underlying world-building defaults become harder to see precisely as they become more powerful.

We propose a corrective lens: \operativeekphrasis\ names prompting as a performative practice in which description is not representation but execution \citep{Bajohr2024OperativeEkphrasis}. Mitchell's \imagetext\ becomes a boundary condition rather than a metaphor: the text/image relation is operationalized inside the model architecture \citep{Mitchell1994EkphrasisOther}. Extending this, we introduce the \worldtext\ to describe the emergent condition where prompts operate as handles on interactive simulation. \thickprompting, in this context, is not \enquote{longer prompts}. It is the layered, itinerant, multimodal procedure by which communities probe model weights to assemble coherent scenes---and, increasingly, coherent worlds \citep{LindleyWhitham2025HolisticPromptCraft}.

To make these claims testable as media practice (not just theory), we turn to the canonical ekphrasis that already describes a world: Homer's Shield of Achilles \citep{Homer1990IliadFagles,HomerIliad18Scaife}. The shield is not an image request; it is a cosmological program. Reading it through \operativeekphrasis\ allows us to do two things at once: (1) show how \thickprompting\ concretely translates text into \imagetext\ and then into an interactive world; and (2) expose how historical rewritings---most starkly Auden's mid-century bureaucratic wasteland---function as competing \enquote{world specifications} whose values and violences can be surfaced, compared, and critiqued \citep{Auden1955ShieldOfAchillesBook,AudenPoetsOrg}.

\section{Operative Ekphrasis and the \texorpdfstring{\imagetext}{imagetext}}

Ekphrasis theory's central paradox has been operationalized. For decades, the field has treated the word--image relation as a rhetorical drama: language reaches toward the visual, fails (or triumphs), and the tension becomes the point \citep{Krieger1992Ekphrasis,Heffernan2004MuseumWords}. Mitchell's \enquote{\imagetext} named that tension: a welded object that both promises control (ekphrastic hope) and threatens erasure (ekphrastic fear) \citep{Mitchell1994EkphrasisOther}. The problem is that this welded object is no longer primarily a metaphor.

In generative image systems, the \imagetext\ is increasingly a built artifact: a prompt-template bound to a probabilistic image generator through a training pipeline that encodes captions, labels, and metadata into model weights. The \enquote{word} does not merely describe the image; it is part of the mechanism by which the image becomes producible at all. That is the ontology shift that ekphrasis discourse cannot politely absorb: the verbal has become executable \citep{Bajohr2024OperativeEkphrasis}.

This is what Bajohr's \operativeekphrasis\ correctly names and what much current discussion still softens into analogy. Prompting is not a new way of talking about images; it is a new way of binding language to a generative apparatus. The decisive feature is not that the prompt is vivid, poetic, or rhetorically skilled. The decisive feature is that the prompt is taken up by a system whose internal structure has been trained to treat language as a handle on the visual.

That handle is not neutral. Because the handle is learned from scraped, captioned culture, it carries cultural defaults as operational constraints: what counts as \enquote{realistic}, \enquote{beautiful}, \enquote{epic}, \enquote{professional}, \enquote{violent}, \enquote{normal}. When the field continues to discuss prompting as if it were traditional ekphrasis---mere description attempting to evoke an image---it commits a category error: it misidentifies the site of agency. The agency is no longer primarily in the literary surface of the description; it is distributed across prompt templates, model weights, platform affordances, and iterative selection loops \citep{BradeEtAl2023Promptify,AlmedaEtAl2024DreamSheets}.

Recognizing this changes what criticism must touch. The prompt cannot be evaluated as a standalone text, because it is only half of an \imagetext\ object whose other half is a generated image emerging from an encoded cultural substrate. This is why claims that \enquote{the prompt is the artwork} are simultaneously plausible and insufficient: the prompt's aesthetic status cannot be separated from the system that operationalizes it, nor from the selection practices that stabilize one output as \enquote{the} output \citep{ChangEtAl2023PromptArtists}.

Manovich's framing of prompting as probing latent space becomes legible here as a concrete mechanism rather than a metaphor: prompting is a structured interrogation of a compressed cultural archive whose responses are probabilistic \citep{ManovichArielli2024ArtificialAesthetics}. The \imagetext, in this sense, is not a single thing you write; it is a coupled system: language operating on weights to produce an image, which then retroacts on language through iterative refinement.

\textbf{Operative ekphrasis therefore forces a hard conclusion:} ekphrasis is no longer only a rhetorical practice; it is now an interface regime. And once ekphrasis becomes an interface regime, the old questions (\enquote{Can words capture images?}) become less urgent than the new ones: Which words bind to which visual outcomes, under whose defaults, with what hidden exclusions, and with what consequences when those bindings scale?

\section{Thick Description Was Never \enquote{More Context.} That Misreading Now Breaks Prompting Research}

\enquote{Thick description} gets domesticated in tech discourse into a harmless slogan: add more context, get better results. That is not Geertz's move. Thick description is a diagnostic weapon: it exposes the invisible codes that make behavior legible, the stacked meanings that make a blink a signal and not just an eyelid twitch \citep{Geertz1993InterpretationCultures}. When prompting borrows the phrase without the bite, it commits the first category error: it treats thickness as verbosity.

World models force the second, more expensive category error: they make \enquote{thickness} operational. A prompt is no longer a request for an image. It is increasingly a \emph{world-spec}---a set of constraints that binds not just what is seen, but what can happen \citep{HaSchmidhuber2018WorldModels,DeepMind2025Genie3Blog,WorldLabs2025MarbleBlog}.

This is the reversal that matters. Thick description interprets a practice that already exists; the culture precedes the text. \thickprompting\ does the opposite: the description executes. The codes are not only read; they are called---summoned out of weights and defaults into visible, navigable form. And because those weights are probabilistic and historically scraped, the \enquote{culture} being invoked is not a stable tradition but a volatile composite: captioned taste, platform priors, market aesthetics, and inherited bias, all hardened into a generative substrate.

That substrate is now being asked to do more than produce \imagetexts. \citet{HaSchmidhuber2018WorldModels} formalize a core premise: a generative model can learn compressed spatiotemporal dynamics and support agents trained inside its own hallucinated \enquote{dream,} later transferred back to an environment. The key point is not reinforcement learning trivia; it is ontological: the model's hallucination is treated as a usable world.

In 2025--2026, that premise is no longer confined to papers. DeepMind's Genie line explicitly frames a general-purpose world model that generates interactive environments from text descriptions \citep{BruceEtAl2024Genie,DeepMind2025Genie3Blog}. In parallel, World Labs' Marble markets high-fidelity, persistent 3D worlds generated from prompts (and other inputs), explicitly describing multimodal world models that reconstruct, generate, and simulate 3D worlds \citep{WorldLabs2025MarbleBlog}.

This is where Mitchell's \imagetext\ stops being the end-state and becomes a stepping-stone. \imagetext\ names a welded object: word--image entanglement as the site of ekphrastic hope/fear \citep{Mitchell1994EkphrasisOther}. \worldtext\ names the new condition: the welded object now includes dynamics---an interactive, probabilistic simulation in which language functions as a handle on scene, object, and behavior. When the output is a world, the prompt is not \enquote{descriptive}. It is constitutional.

That shift detonates today's evaluation norms. Accuracy, similarity, and one-shot \enquote{did you get what you wanted?} metrics collapse when the artifact is a navigable environment. A world is not \enquote{correct} because it matches a reference image; it is operative because it sustains coherence, affordances, and constraint under interaction. The prompt is therefore not merely an instruction---it is a governance mechanism: it defines what the user can do, what the world will permit, what kinds of beings and events are even representable.

So the stakes of \thickprompting\ are not primarily aesthetic finesse. The stakes are legibility and power: who gets to write the world-spec, which defaults get smuggled in as \enquote{natural,} and what becomes invisible as world models pursue seamlessness. If the \imagetext\ already threatened to dissolve the word into the image, the \worldtext\ threatens to dissolve critique into experience---because a convincing world is the most efficient way to stop people from asking how it was made.

\section{The \enquote{Qualities} of Thick Prompting Are Not Traits --- They Are Control Points}

\thickprompting\ is usually described like a bag of clever techniques. That framing is strategically soothing. The actual pattern is harsher: every \enquote{quality} of \thickprompting\ marks a place where agency moved out of the sentence and into a broader compilation pipeline---templates, reference media, toolchains, and selection loops \citep{LindleyWhitham2025HolisticPromptCraft}. Once world models enter the picture---interactive environments generated from text prompts---the cost of misreading these control points goes up, because the prompt stops specifying an image and starts specifying a world-behavior envelope \citep{DeepMind2025Genie3Blog,WorldLabs2025MarbleBlog}.

\subsection*{Iterative $\rightarrow$ Selection is the hidden author}
Iteration is not refinement; it is search plus curation. The loop does not merely \enquote{improve prompts}---it trains the human into the model's priors by rewarding what the model will reliably give back. This is why evaluation that scores only the final prompt-output pair is structurally blind: it ignores the authoring force of repeated sampling, rejection, and convergence \citep{BradeEtAl2023Promptify,AlmedaEtAl2024DreamSheets}.

\subsection*{Multimodal $\rightarrow$ Reference inputs become covert steering}
Once images/video enter the loop, \enquote{prompting} becomes closer to constraint injection than description. Multimodal inputs do not just add context; they anchor composition, lighting, and subject-matter in ways that evade text-based auditing \citep{LindleyWhitham2025HolisticPromptCraft}. In world-building systems marketed as multimodal world models (e.g., Marble), this steering becomes a world-specification primitive, not a stylistic flourish \citep{WorldLabs2025MarbleBlog}.

\subsection*{Heterogeneous $\rightarrow$ The prompt becomes a program}
When schemas, scripts, tool calls, or UI state determine what gets sent, the \enquote{prompt} is no longer a string---it is compiled behavior. Auditing \enquote{the prompt} without auditing the pipeline becomes like reviewing a film by grading the clapperboard.

\subsection*{Itinerant $\rightarrow$ Portability is a lie; prompts drift across platforms}
A prompt that \enquote{works} in one model/platform is not a stable instruction---it is a negotiated handshake with a specific latent culture plus interface grammar. As prompts travel from text-to-image to text-to-3D to interactive world systems, they undergo semantic drift because each platform binds language to different priors, affordances, and failure modes.

\subsection*{Performative \& interactive $\rightarrow$ The artifact is the trajectory, not the output}
Interactive prompting makes authorship a time-series: the system answers, the human corrects, the system re-answers---until the loop stabilizes into an artifact. That means the relevant object for critique is not \enquote{prompt + image,} but the conversation plus state plus iteration path that produced the result. \citet{HaSchmidhuber2018WorldModels} make the implication explicit: agents can be trained inside generated \enquote{dreams} and then transferred---i.e., the simulated environment's dynamics become part of what is learned.

\textbf{Therefore:} the \enquote{qualities} of \thickprompting\ are not a taxonomy of prompter skill. They are the exposed joints of a new interface regime where templates behave like laws, defaults behave like ideology, and iteration behaves like an invisible editor. As world models push interactive world generation from text further into public-facing prototypes, the central question is no longer \enquote{How do we write better prompts?} It is: which compilation pipelines are we letting write our worlds---and what are they making impossible by default?

\section{The Shield(s) of Achilles Is the Benchmark That Breaks the Current Prompt Paradigm}

Everyone agrees the Shield of Achilles is \enquote{the} canonical ekphrasis. That consensus is precisely the trap: it encourages readers to treat the shield as a literary ornament---an image described in words---rather than what it actually is: a world specification embedded inside an epic \citep{Homer1990IliadFagles,HomerIliad18Scaife}.

That matters now because world models are arriving with the exact same structure, but with an interface. Genie-style systems explicitly frame interactive environments generated from text descriptions \citep{BruceEtAl2024Genie,DeepMind2025Genie3Blog}. Marble markets the same move---promptable, persistent 3D worlds---and positions image-to-world lifting as a standard workflow \citep{WorldLabs2025MarbleBlog}.

So the shield is not an \enquote{example} of our argument. It is the stress test that reveals where today's prompting discourse fails. The shield forces an upgrade in the unit of analysis: the prompt is not a sentence that requests a picture; the prompt is a structured procedure that binds language to world-making.

Homer's shield passage (Iliad 18) already behaves like a generator: it enumerates cosmology, agriculture, dance, adjudication, war---multiple registers of social reality---then constrains them inside a circular topology bounded by Ocean \citep{HomerIliad18Scaife}. In other words, it does what contemporary world systems promise: it does not merely render an object; it compiles a total environment with internal contrasts and rules.

\subsection{The workflow is the argument (why thickness lives in the pipeline)}
If the field keeps looking for \enquote{thickness} in longer prose prompts, it will miss the actual location of control: the compiled workflow. Our \texttt{ACHILLES // OPTICAL\_GRID\_v7} artifact makes the point with cruelty:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.25em]
  \item the user does not \enquote{write a prompt} so much as select a coordinate in a topological text-world (zone selection on a UV-mapped sphere);
  \item the system does not \enquote{use the stanza} so much as extract a stochastic micro-spec (five to eight-word contiguous snippets);
  \item the output is not \enquote{returned} so much as re-inscribed into the same navigable surface, becoming persistent world texture.
\end{itemize}
That is not promptcraft as rhetoric. That is promptcraft as procedural world-binding. Once you admit this, you can stop pretending the prompt is a string. The prompt is: geometry plus selection rules plus wrapper template plus model call plus compositing plus persistence. The \enquote{text} is only one operand in a larger compilation graph.

\subsection{Two competing futures: seamless world models vs.\ exposed world-making}
World-model development strongly incentivizes a single aesthetic endpoint: the invisible interface. \enquote{Holodeck} design treats the prompt as a hidden trigger and rewards systems that bury their own mediation under photoreal continuity \citep{DeepMind2025Genie3Blog}. Our ACHILLES artifact argues the opposite: exposure is the aesthetic. CRT overlays, shutter flashes, targeting HUDs, blend-mode toggles, UV-map exports---these are not flourishes. They are governance. They keep the prompt visible as an operational negotiation between human text and machine weights.

This is not a stylistic preference; it is an epistemic fork. The more seamless world generation becomes, the more the world's defaults become illegible. The composite stream refuses that illegibility by making compilation perceptible.

\subsection{Homer vs.\ Auden as a controlled collision of world-specs}
Auden's \textit{The Shield of Achilles} is not \enquote{inspired by} Homer in a soft way; it is an overwrite. First published in 1952 and later collected in \textit{The Shield of Achilles} (1955), Auden replaces Homer's plural, vibrant social totality with a barren, bureaucratic landscape---a world where procedure remains but meaning drains out \citep{Auden1955ShieldOfAchillesBook,AudenPoetsOrg}.

\operativeekphrasis\ turns this into a lab condition: two world-specifications compete for instantiation. Homer specifies a cosmos of stacked human activities; Auden specifies the modern administrative void. When a generative system is asked to \enquote{make the shield,} it is forced to negotiate between these competing cultural weights---between abundance and austerity, ritual and bureaucracy, \textit{polis} and apparatus. That negotiation becomes the real object of critique, because it reveals which history the model is predisposed to render as \enquote{natural.}

\subsection{The claim this benchmark forces}
If the shield is treated as a benchmark, one conclusion becomes unavoidable: image generation was never the endpoint. The endpoint is world generation, and the real contest is whether our interfaces will conceal or expose the compilation of \worldtexts. That is why the Shield of Achilles belongs here: it is the oldest \worldtext\ in the Western archive, and it still outclasses our current evaluation habits---because it refuses to be \enquote{just an image.}

\section{Conclusion: Seamless Worlds Are the Default --- and That Default Is the Problem}

The field's accepted trajectory is clear: make the world model better until the interface vanishes. Genie-style systems explicitly frame interactive environments that can be explored in real time from simple text descriptions \citep{DeepMind2025Genie3Blog}. Marble markets the same destination---high-fidelity, persistent 3D worlds from short prompts---i.e., world generation as a consumer workflow \citep{WorldLabs2025MarbleBlog}.

That is the paradigm we have to destabilize: prompt-disappearance is not a neutral UX win. It is an epistemic and political design choice. The more seamless the world, the more the world's defaults become law while looking like nature.

Mitchell's \imagetext\ named the old anxiety: the word longs to govern the image, then fears being consumed by it \citep{Mitchell1994EkphrasisOther}. \worldtext\ is that anxiety upgraded into infrastructure: language does not just \enquote{describe} a world---it binds to a generative apparatus that produces a navigable simulation whose dynamics can train agents, shape perception, and normalize cultural priors as environmental constraint \citep{HaSchmidhuber2018WorldModels,DeepMind2025Genie3Blog}. In this sense, Gibson's \enquote{consensual hallucination} becomes a design brief: the hallucination is no longer only representational; it is interactive and consequential \citep{Gibson1984Neuromancer}.

So the core risk is not merely \enquote{people might make fake images.} The core risk is metric capture plus illegibility: evaluation regimes reward coherence and immersion while ignoring provenance, defaults, exclusions, and the compiled workflow that actually authored the world.

This is why the Shield of Achilles is not a charming example; it is a warning label. Homer's shield is a world-spec---cosmos, city, conflict, labor---bounded by Ocean \citep{HomerIliad18Scaife}. Auden's rewrite is also a world-spec, but it compiles modern bureaucracy and void into the same formal container \citep{Auden1955ShieldOfAchillesBook}. \operativeekphrasis\ lets us treat those as competing specifications whose consequences can be staged, compared, and audited---rather than treated as \enquote{interpretations} floating above mechanism \citep{Bajohr2024OperativeEkphrasis}.

\textbf{Therefore, the conclusion is a design mandate:} if world models push toward the photoreal stream (seamless immersion), criticism must push toward the composite stream (exposed compilation). Our ACHILLES artifact is not just an artwork; it is a governance prototype: it keeps the word visible, dramatizes extraction, and reveals the UV underbelly so the \enquote{world} cannot pretend it arrived unmade.

\subsection*{Non-negotiables for worldtext criticism}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.25em]
  \item Treat the prompt as a compiled workflow, not a string: selection rules, templates, reference inputs, iteration path, and compositing are part of the authoring object.
  \item Evaluate auditability, not just realism: can a viewer recover how the world was specified, sampled, and stabilized?
  \item Expose defaults as \enquote{world laws}: style priors, safety filters, dataset bias, and platform affordances must be legible at the point of experience.
  \item Preserve the prompt-trail: the time-series of interaction is the artifact when the output is a world, not a frame.
  \item Prefer accentuated interfaces when stakes are cultural/historical: seamlessness is not innocence; it is concealment.
\end{itemize}

If we do not institutionalize those requirements, the \worldtext\ will become the most efficient machine ever built for laundering ideology into \enquote{environment.} If we do, \operativeekphrasis\ becomes what it should have been all along: not magic, but visible world-making under constraint.

